# Rank 1675 / CVE-2024-49761 (GHSA-2rxp-v6pw-ch6m): REXML's base parser
# unescapes entity references in parsed XML with a static regex whose `0*`
# prefix and whose first alternation branch `\\d+` can both consume the same
# run of `0` characters, so the engine must retry every split of that run --
# a ReDoS quadratic in the number of digits between `&#` and `x...;`
# (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression complexity, CWE-400).
#
# `Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES` (lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb:153 at the
# fix parent) is `/&#0*((?:\\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/`, and
# `BaseParser#unnormalize` applies it to the string it is handed with a
# global `rv.gsub!(Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES) { ... }` (baseparser.rb:571).
# The string is parsed XML content, which is the input an XML parser exists to
# accept, so the cost is entirely the attacker's; the advisory's trigger is
# "many digits between &# and x...; in a hex numeric character reference", and
# only Ruby 3.1's regexp engine is affected. The fixed revision removes the `0*` prefix
# entirely: leading zeros before `x` are invalid XML character references
# anyway (the added regression test asserts `&#x61;&#0x61;` unescapes to the
# literal text `a&#0x61;`), and a digit run then has exactly one consumer --
# `\\d+` alone -- instead of two overlapping ones.
#
# The pool row is correct in every field: ce59f2eb1aeb is a real, readable,
# single-library-file commit plus its regression test ("parser: fix a bug that
# &#0x...; is accepted as a character reference"), the vulnerable regex with
# the `0*` prefix is present at its parent, the remediation (no `0*`, and the
# simplified block that parses the `x` prefix explicitly) is present at the
# commit itself, and the v3.3.9 release tag contains it. .
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap -- the same classifier family rank 1648 recorded for
# the rails-html-sanitizer url() regex, re-measured here rather than inherited,
# with a different specific trigger: this pattern's ambiguity is a *run-split*
# between a prefix quantifier and a nested class branch, not the polynomial
# backtracking of an unanchored lazy class.
#
# What fires today, measured. `vyql match` over the real
# lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb at the vulnerable revision reports no label of any
# kind at :153 (the definition) or :571 (the gsub); the only sink labels in
# the file are `code.RegexMatch`/`code.RegexMatchSink` on the two *interpolated*
# literals at :111 and :147, both of which contain `(?:#{ATTDEF})*`, a repeated
# group with a repeat inside. Bounded scans (`--cache off --max-ram 6GB`) of
# the full checkout at both revisions report 98 findings each and the two
# finding sets are byte-identical, with nothing on baseparser.rb at all.
#
# The diagnosis: `rubyRegexMayBacktrack` (internal/extract/frontend/
# treesitter/ruby.go:1152) answers false for this literal, so it lowers to a
# bare `nir.Const` (ruby.go:1078) and no `__regex.match` call node exists for
# `regexMatch205` (vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql:1125) to label. Under it,
# `regexambig.Ambiguous` returns false because the pattern has neither a
# repeated group containing a repeat nor two *sibling* single-character
# repeats: `regexSplitsOneCharRun` (internal/extract/regexambig/
# regexambig.go:616) scans one branch's top-level atoms only, and the second
# consumer of the zero-run -- `\\d+` -- is nested inside the *unquantified*
# alternation group `((?:\\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+))`, so the atom scan sees only
# the group (a non-repeat) and stops at it. `rubyRegexMayBacktrack`'s own
# same-quantified-literal-in-two-branches check splits at the `|` inside the
# group and finds one quantified literal. The overlap computation the shape
# needs exists in the package (`regexRepeatOverlapsContinuation`,
# regexambig.go:425) but only uses to *withdraw* a report during
# disambiguation, never to raise one.
#
# The binding route is measured and is expressible but not shippable. A probe
# binding keying `0*((?:` in `node.context.literal` fired at the module-scope
# and all three nested class-scope presence nodes of a repro of the real code,
# so the pattern text is reachable at module and class scope and a text binding
# discriminates 1 -> 0 against the patched literal. It fails genericity: the
# only key a text binding can hold is this CVE's own spelling. The same
# run-split written `\\d*(?:\\d+|...)`, `[0-9]*((?:[0-9]+)|...)`, or with any
# other leading character is invisible to it, and the module/class scope
# clause is itself an artifact of this project placing the regex in
# `module Private` -- rank 1648's same-family pattern sat inline in a method
# body, in function scope. Enumerated across the campaign's clones (14,368
# non-test .rb files, 10,627 unique regex literals): a single unescaped
# literal character carrying a backtracking quantifier followed by an
# overlapping unbounded continuation occurs exactly once -- this CVE's own
# literal -- so the needle list is the incident's fingerprint, not a weakness
# class. That is AGENTS.md's genericity test failing, and it is the family
# outcome ranks 1343 and 1648 measured (the discriminator is not in the
# pattern text).
#
# The engine fix this rank would need is a new classification trigger in the
# shared leaf package -- promote `regexRepeatOverlapsContinuation` to a report
# trigger for a single-char backtracking repeat whose continuation's first set
# contains that character, or descend into unquantified alternation groups in
# `regexSplitsOneCharRun`. Either changes which regexes are called ambiguous
# for every language sharing the package (the JS frontend at
# javascript.go:1113 and the shared lowering at lowering.go:2163), and reverses
# a precision restriction the code documents in place ("Restricted to
# single-character sets ... flagging those reports most real-world regexes").
# Not mechanical. A second, independent blocker sits on the taint route even
# after classification: the pattern is defined as a module constant 400 lines
# from its use, so a classified literal produces its node at the definition
# with no edge to the consuming `gsub!`, and -- as rank 1648 measured with an
# isolating pair -- a regex supplied as a call argument lowers to an
# `__regex.match` node whose only argument is the pattern text, with no link
# to the receiver it is applied to. Only the presence route (VYQL-DOS-003 at
# the definition site) could ever report this spelling.
#
# The isolating pair below holds the source fixed and varies only how the
# second consumer of the zero-run is spelled: a sibling single-character
# repeat, which the classifier sees today, versus the digit class inside the
# unquantified alternation group, which it does not.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1675: the character-reference regex splits a zero run between the 0* prefix and the nested digit branch"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  module REXML
    module Parsers
      class BaseParser
        module Private
          CHARACTER_REFERENCES = /&#0*((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        end
        private_constant :Private

        def unnormalize( string, entities=nil, filter=nil )
          rv = string.dup
          matches = rv.scan( REFERENCE_RE )
          return rv if matches.size == 0
          rv.gsub!( Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES ) {
            m=$1
            m = "0#{m}" if m[0] == ?x
            [Integer(m)].pack('U*')
          }
          rv
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675: the fixed regex leaves one consumer for a digit run and the block parses the x prefix explicitly"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  module REXML
    module Parsers
      class BaseParser
        module Private
          CHARACTER_REFERENCES = /&#((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        end
        private_constant :Private

        def unnormalize( string, entities=nil, filter=nil )
          rv = string.dup
          matches = rv.scan( REFERENCE_RE )
          return rv if matches.size == 0
          rv.gsub!( Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES ) {
            m=$1
            if m.start_with?("x")
              code_point = Integer(m[1..-1], 16)
            else
              code_point = Integer(m, 10)
            end
            [code_point].pack('U*')
          }
          rv
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the same run-split with the second consumer as a sibling single-char repeat is reported"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  class CharRef
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /&#0*0+;/
        "a"
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the CVE's spelling, the digit class inside the unquantified alternation group, is not reported"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  class CharRef
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /&#0*((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        "a"
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the fixed spelling keeps the alternation and drops the 0* prefix"
  lang ruby
